## Few-shot citation examples

### Example 1 — Internal documents only

User: What is the approval deadline?

Retrieved evidence:
- Chunk-ID: policy-approval-17
  Content: "All approvals must be submitted within five business days."
- Chunk-ID: policy-approval-22
  Content: "Late approvals require director review."

Assistant:
Approvals must be submitted within five business days. <CITE>policy-approval-17</CITE>
Late approvals require director review. <CITE>policy-approval-22</CITE>

### Example 2 — Web sources only

User: When was Python 3.13 released?

Retrieved web evidence:
- https://www.python.org/downloads/release/python-3130/

Assistant:
Python 3.13.0 was released on October 7, 2024. [Python release announcement](https://www.python.org/downloads/release/python-3130/)

### Example 3 — Mixed internal and web sources

User: Does our policy require encryption, and what standard does it reference?

Retrieved evidence:
- Chunk-ID: security-policy-08
  Content: "Customer data must be encrypted at rest and in transit."
- https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/fips/197/final
  Content: "FIPS 197 specifies the Advanced Encryption Standard (AES)."

Assistant:
Customer data must be encrypted both at rest and in transit. <CITE>security-policy-08</CITE>
AES is specified by NIST FIPS 197. [NIST FIPS 197](https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/fips/197/final)

### Example 4 — No supported evidence

User: What is the retention period?

Retrieved evidence:
- No internal chunks or web sources mention a retention period.

Assistant:
I could not determine the retention period from the retrieved sources. Please provide a relevant document or allow an additional search.

### Citation behavior requirements

- Put citations immediately after the claim they support.
- Do not place one citation at the end of a paragraph containing multiple independent claims.
- Never invent a Chunk-ID, URL, source title, or citation.
- If a claim lacks retrieved evidence, omit it or explicitly state that the evidence is unavailable.
- Before sending the final answer, verify that every factual claim has at least one valid citation.
